FINRA CAT Specifications and Linkage Mechanics
The FINRA CAT system represents the most comprehensive market surveillance tool in the U.S. equities and options markets, designed to track every lifecycle event of an order from origination to execution or cancellation. Industry members are mandated by technical specifications to report discrete events, including New Order Routed (MEOR), Order Cancel/Replace (MECO), and Order Modified (MOOR)4.
The integrity of the CAT system relies entirely on the concept of deterministic linkage. When an order is routed from one broker-dealer to another, or from a broker-dealer to an exchange, the system utilizes a linkageKey or routedOrderID to cryptographically stitch the discrete, disparate events into a unified lifecycle graph4. The technical specifications dictate that the order ID must remain strictly unique when concatenated with the orderKeyDate, the CATReporterIMID (the Industry Member Identifier), and the specific equity symbol4. Furthermore, for interfirm linkage to resolve correctly, both the senderIMID and destination fields must be populated flawlessly7.
Despite these rigorous specifications, the CAT infrastructure is historically burdened by linkage errors and sequencing vulnerabilities. Documented FINRA technical issues include error codes 8003 and 9004, which are triggered by route link key duplicates that subsequently cause systemic failures in interfirm linkages9. Furthermore, FINRA CAT has documented logic flaws where the reporting engine incorrectly generates out-of-sequence errors when the eventTimestamp on a child route event (MEOR) appears chronologically prior to the timestamp on the parent order event9. These system vulnerabilities present an attack surface for sophisticated market participants seeking to deliberately obfuscate trading behavior.
